Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Lancer**, a bipedal marksman-mech with a heavy rail-lance
cannon mounted on one shoulder — a turret bolted to its frame that elevates on its
trunnion and kicks back along its own axis — while it strides on two legs, as a
{{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by
**painting discrete voxel cells** one operation at a time, and you **author its walk
and weapon animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Lancer is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the mech into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape
every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a walking, aiming
marksman-mech needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json`
— together with the rig you define are your submission.

You paint **discrete opaque voxel cells**. Set and clear single cells, fill and
stroke boxes, draw 3D lines and spheres, and use a mirror plane. Voxels are
**opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. The volume starts **empty**.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `voxel-anim define-part`
(giving each a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and
author the required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel
operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you paint
into it; each part is sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. Each call only
records the operation to that part's log and renders **nothing** — run `voxel-anim
render` to (re)draw the previews and the assembled scene (`--component <part>`
redraws one part; `--time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at an
instant of an animation), and you **must** run `voxel-anim render` before you finish
so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry the run is scored from — an unrendered part
is scored as empty.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name torso
voxel-anim fill-box --part torso --x0 1 --y0 1 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 5 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and defining animations and their
keyframes), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags —
that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read `parts/<part>.png` after you run
`voxel-anim render`** to see that part's progress, **and read the assembled-scene previews under
`scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** —
the whole mech composed from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The
scene views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together: that the legs seat
under the body and reach the ground, and the cannon seats on its shoulder mount with
its barrel clearing the hull through the full elevation. Because each
part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it
belongs on the finished mech; the scene simply composes them there.

